WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.190
Our faculty found that students who used

2
00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:05.750
the tutoring centre had lower grades.

3
00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.620
A dean proposed closing it.

4
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.800
Before anyone voted, I asked: who

5
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.460
chooses to go to tutoring?

6
00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.890
Today: why correlation misleads, how a

7
00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.750
causal diagram tells you what to

8
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.470
adjust for, and how

9
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.930
difference-in-differences estimates an

10
00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:25.230
effect.

11
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.950
A confounder causes both the

12
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.410
treatment and the outcome.

13
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.840
Here, prior struggle sends students to

14
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.080
tutoring and also lowers grades.

15
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.290
The raw comparison mixes the effect of

16
00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.410
tutoring with the effect of struggling.

17
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.830
Pearl's causal diagrams make

18
00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.150
assumptions explicit.

19
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.990
Arrows mean direct causes.

20
00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:52.290
To estimate an effect, block every

21
00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.570
back-door path by adjusting for

22
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.210
confounders.

23
00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.850
But never adjust for a

24
00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.750
collider or a mediator.

25
00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.970
One department added tutoring in a

26
00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.870
new term; another did not.

27
00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.350
The first rose from sixty to seventy.

28
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.100
The second rose from sixty

29
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.140
two to sixty six anyway.

30
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.580
Some of that rise would have

31
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:16.960
happened without tutoring.

32
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.380
Difference-in-differences subtracts

33
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.480
the comparison group's change from

34
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.760
the treated group's change.

35
00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.490
Ten minus four gives an

36
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.030
effect of six points.

37
00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.890
It assumes both groups would have moved

38
00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.570
in parallel without the treatment.

39
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.100
Students learn causal reasoning best

40
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.760
when they must defend a decision.

41
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.150
Give them a real policy question,

42
00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.950
ask them to draw the diagram first,

43
00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.170
and grade the assumptions they

44
00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.230
state, not only the number.

45
00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:51.910
Quick check!

46
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.640
Tutoring affects study hours, and

47
00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.900
study hours affect grades.

48
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.830
To estimate the total effect of

49
00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:02.990
tutoring on grades, should we

50
00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.650
adjust for study hours?

51
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:06.300
No.

52
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.990
Study hours are a mediator,

53
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.750
part of how tutoring works.

54
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.510
Adjusting for them removes some of the

55
00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.970
very effect we want to measure.

56
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.760
In the app, open the scatter lab.

57
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.380
Look at marks against study hours,

58
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.380
read the correlation, then ask which

59
00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.700
confounder, such as prior grade,

60
00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.300
could create the same pattern.

61
00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:30.250
Your turn.

62
00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:34.430
Treated group: from fifty to fifty eight.

63
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.930
Comparison group: from

64
00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.390
fifty two to fifty five.

65
00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:41.390
What is the

66
00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:43.870
difference-in-differences estimate?

67
00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.090
Five points: eight minus three.

68
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:51.640
Remember: draw the diagram, adjust for

69
00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.640
confounders but not mediators, and use a

70
00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.880
comparison group with parallel trends.

71
00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.540
Try it in your next lecture: give teams

72
00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.620
one news headline claiming a cause, ask

73
00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.560
them to draw the causal diagram.

74
00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:07.950
Then have them name one

75
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.670
confounder the article ignored.
